0 @N14@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT CHARLES DAVID GIBSON MACK, M. D.— Prominent among the successful men of the medical profession in Middlesex County, is Dr. Charles David Gibson Mack, who has been engaged in general practice here since 1918. Dr. Mack is a graduate of the Medica
1 CONC l School of Boston University, and is a veteran of the World War, in which he served from 1914 to 1918, first with the British Navy and later with the American Army, as surgeon. He is a thirty-second degree Mason, and has a host of friends in Middl
1 CONC esex County. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Dr. Charles David Gibson Mack was born in East Boston, August 3, 1873, son of William B. Mack, an inventor, who was born in England, and who invented the first locomotive injector ever used on any railroad, and of Helen (Duff) Mack, who was born i
1 CONC n Paisley, Scotland. Dr. Mack received his early and preparatory education in the public schools of East Boston, and after graduation matriculated in the Medical School of Boston University, from which he was graduated in 1895, with the degree of D
1 CONC octor of Medicine. He engaged in general practice in Boston, but later located in Tremont Street, Dover Street, then Huntington Avenue. When the World War began in 1914, he enlisted in the British Navy, as a surgeon, and served on one of His Majest
1 CONC y’s Troop Ships for two years and fourteen days, and on the “Arcobo” one year. At the end of that time, in 1917, he enlisted in the American Army and served with the 26th Infantry, Third Battalion, First Division, until 1918, when he was must
1 CONC ered' out of service. Upon his return to civilian life he located in Somerville, Massachusetts, where he has since been engaged in the practice of medicine, specifically in the line of high blood pressure and hernia. He is a member of the Masonic O
1 CONC rder, being a member of all the Scottish Rite bodies, in which he has attained the thirty-second degree, and he is also a member of the Ancient Arabic Order Nobles of the Mystic Shrine. He is a member of the Army and Navy Club, and of the Delbosy P
1 CONC ost, Veterans of Foreign Wars, and the Sojournist Masonic Club. His religious affiliation is with the Park Street Methodist Episcopal Church of Somerville. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Dr. Charles David Gibson Mack was married, in New York City, July 24, 1923, to Gertrude S. Dennison, who was born in Dorchester, Massachusetts, daughter of George and Gertrude A. Dennison, the last-mentioned of whom is deceased. Dr. Mack’s offic
1 CONC e is located at No. 22 Bromfield Road, West Somerville.
