0 @N534@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Walter Ker (1656-1748) with other Scotch nonconformists, was deprived of his property and exiled under perpetual banishment upon the accession of James II in 1685.  He migrated from Glasgow, Scotland in September, 1685 and arrived in New Jersey abo
1 CONC ut December, 1685. One of the founding fathers of the town of Freehold, New Jersey. by SallyWilson9
1 CONT "Walter Ker and the “Sweet Singers”" by Douglas W. Somerset https://www.biblicalstudies.org.uk/pdf/srshj/02_085.pdf
1 CONT Walter Ker was a Scottish Covenanter who was banished to East Jersey in 1685, was instrumental in the setting up of Presbyterianism in America, and lived to see the revivals there under William Tennent, David Brainerd and others in the 1730s and 17
1 CONC 40s. This article
1 CONT looks at the first part of Walter Ker’s life, when he was in Scotland and was associated with the fanatical group called the Gibbites or “Sweet Singers”.1 The story of the Gibbites is one of the curiosities of Scottish Church history.
1 CONT 1. Ker joins the “Sweet Singers”
1 CONT Walter Ker was born in Torphichen in Linlithgowshire, Scotland, in 1656 or 1657.2 Nothing is known of his parentage or early years, but presumably he was brought up in a Covenanting family. He was present at the Battle of Bothwell Bridge in June 16
1 CONC 79, and about that time he was employed as a servant by Marion MacCulloch (d. 1690), the second wife
