1 CONT Will of Peter Watson, of freehold, Mon. Co. "Being In perfit health" Dated Feb 25, 1725-26. Proved by John Whitlock, Junr one of the Evidences, who saw other evidences sign; before Mich Kearny, Surro, Amboy, Oct 17, 1727
1 CONT Gives: to my Eldist son William Watson and his heirs, three hundred Acres of Land to be measured to him Lying upon the - East end of my farm next to William parents upon Mil- - Brook Including the hovfe barn orcherd and all other Ap-tances there un
1 CONC to belonging. . . all the REmainder of that plantation to my son Richard Watson and his heirs. . . to my son Gawvin Watson and his heirs all the . . . Land belonging to me Lying between himself and James Edwards. . . all my Intrest to the meeting h
1 CONC ovfe and Land belonging unto my four sons Equally. . . to my Loving Wife Agnes Watson all the Land belonging to me Lying between Gravele Brook and Peter Tices Line and Injoyning all the Hovfres barns, orcherds, meadows both salt and fresh with al
1 CONC l appertenances. . . dyring her natural life and after hear death to my son David Watson and his heirs pyaing to her the sum of fifty pounds and in Case he the said David Watson do refuse to pay to her the fifty pounds, I leave her power to sell
1 CONC . . . Either Land or meadows as much as will make her the fifty pounds and to grant deeds or bills of sales for the same. . . To Agnes, my wife, my two Negros Jack & - - to seel . . . as . .- - - all the rest of my moveables to Agnes my wife to dis
1 CONC pose of them as she thinks fit. . . 
1 CONT Appoints: Loving wife Agnes Watson my whole and sole executor
1 CONT Signed Peter Watson
1 CONT Witnesses
1 CONT Thomas Whitlock
1 CONT John Whitlock Jr
1 CONT Walter Wall
1 CONT Oath of Executrix Agnes Watson; before Mich Kearny, Surr., Amboy Oct 17, 1727
1 CONT Recorded in the Book of Will B, . . .
0 @N670@ NOTE New Aberdeen, Or, The Scotch Settlement of Monmouth County, New Jersey, p 2
1 CONT 
1 CONT Freehold Township erected in 1693, had for its easterly boundary a line that came so near to Matawan village as the meeting of "Gravel" and "Watson's brook" (which latter has sometimes been called Aberdeen creek) and thence to Chesequake creek, a
1 CONC t that ime included in Monmouth. Peter Watson, who owned a plantation of several hundred acres on Watson's brook, including what is now Freneau and the old Mount Pleasant Presbyterian graveyard, was himself a Scotchman and a Presbyterian. In Augus
1 CONC t 1683, David Barclay sent to East Jersey by the Ship Exchange, joint stock for his own and other proprietors advantage. John Hanton or Hamton and John Reid were overseers and Peter Watson and James Reid were seervants. The servants were to remai
1 CONC n for four years from their first time, (N.J. Archives Vol I, p 465)  Each servant was to have according to his "indenture's twenty-five acres, a tradesmen of them 30 paying 2 d. an acre," etc.  Peter Watson seems to have been a son in law of Alexa
1 CONC nder Napier. His will dated February 25, 1726, names his wife, Agnes and his sons William, Gawen and David; but his father-in-law, Alexander Napier, in his will (1744) mentions his granddaughters Agnes Watson, Elizabeth Watson and Margaret Watson
1 CONC . Captain Abrahm Watson buried at the age of 67 in 1756 in Middeltown (village) Presbyterian Graveyard, is believed, however to have been the son of Peter's cousin John Watwon to whom Peter wrote urging his removed to East Jersey in August 1684. Pe
1 CONC ter's brother also lived in Scotland and was likewise urged by Peter to migrate to Jersey. Peter Watson with his friend and fellow servant, James Reid, was settled at Amboy on a farm belonging to the proprietors and there they probably served thei
1 CONC r full time of four years until "about the month of December 1687" (N.J. Arch., I, p 468)
1 CONT 
1 CONT https://electricscotland.com/history/america/newaberdeen.pdf
