0 @N1030@ NOTE === Inscription on Walter Ker’s gravestone ===
1 CONT Here lies what’s Mortal of Walter Ker
1 CONT Deceased June 10th 1748 in ye 92 year of his Age
1 CONT who long with Patience Bore life’s heavy load
1 CONT willing to spend & to be spent for God
1 CONT the noble Portrait in a line to paint
1 CONT he Breath’d a Father liv’d & dy’d a saint
1 CONT Here sleeps in peace the aged sire’s dust
1 CONT Till the glad Trump arouse the sleeping Just
0 @N1031@ NOTE === The Battle of Bothwell Bridge, or Bothwell Brig' ===
1 CONT  took place on 22 June 1679. It was fought between government troops and militant Presbyterian Covenanters, and signaled the end of their brief rebellion. The battle took place at the bridge over the River Clyde between Hamilton and Bothwell in Lan
1 CONC arkshire, Scotland. The battlefield has been included in the Inventory of Historic Battlefields in Scotland and protected by Historic Scotland under the Historic Environment (Amendment) Act 2011. wikipedia.org
0 @N1032@ NOTE === Relationship ===
1 CONT 7th Great Grandfather
0 @N1033@ NOTE === Unlikely father for of Joseph Kerr ===
1 CONT KER WALTER -Wrong last name and he would have been 77 yearS old when Joseph  Kerr was born.
0 @N1034@ NOTE === Family crest is pictured at right. ===
1 CONT Family crest is pictured at right.
0 @N1035@ NOTE === Sons ===
1 CONT William, Samuel, Joseph and John noted on page 278 in the Old Tennent Church History
0 @N1036@ NOTE === Migrated from Glasgow, Scotland, in Sept ===
1 CONT Migrated from Glasgow, Scotland, in September, 1685, and arrived in New Jersey about December, 1685.  One of the founding fathers of the town of Hopewell(?), New Jersey. Inscription at Old Tennent Church:  "Here lies whats mortal of Walter Ker dece
1 CONC ased June 10, 1748 in 92 year of his age who long with patience bore lifes heavy load willing to spend & be spent for God. the noble pourvoir in a time to pain the . . . breached a father lived and died a saint.  Here sleeps in peace the aged sire'
1 CONC s dust til the glad trump arouse the sleeping just."  Copied by 6gg son, Theodore Joseph Naylor. See _The Kerr Clan of New Jersey_ (subtitled, Beginning with Walter Ker of Freehold and including Other Related Lines) compiled by William C. Armstrong
1 CONC . Ker's death date & age at death taken from his tombstone "on a hilly point one-half mile east of the present Tennent church building." per Symmes _History of Old Tennent Church, tombstone inscription:  "Walter Ker, d. June 10, 1748, in 92nd yr.
0 @N1037@ NOTE === !* HISTORY *
1 CONT This man was a Covenanter,  ===
1 CONT !* HISTORY *
1 CONT This man was a Covenanter, got imprisoned and banished to America in 1685.
1 CONT He was known as "The Father of Old Tennent Church" in Freehold, Monmouth NJ.
1 CONT His home was used as a Hospital during the Battle of Monmouth.
1 CONT 
1 CONT !* OTHER SOURCES *
1 CONT This information from Marilyn Smith in letter of 4 APR 1989.
0 @N1038@ NOTE === Emigrated ===
1 CONT Walter Ker was banished for life to the colonies in Sept 1685 for refusing to sign the Oath of Allegiance to
1 CONT  King James II. Walter became on the original settlers of Freehold, Monmouth, NJ. 
1 CONT Walter Ker arrived on the ship O'Henry and Francisco in 1685. Walter was one of 200 passegers which left from the port of Leith near Edinburg, Sotland
