0 @N1056@ NOTE === History of the Watson Family ===
1 CONT Newspapers by Ancestry
1 CONT Article clipped from The Freehold Transcript and The Monmouth Inquirer
1 CONT Freehold, New Jersey- Friday June 26, 1931
1 CONT 
1 CONT Peter Watson (3) landed on Staten Island, December 19, 1683, having been brought out under a four-year indenture. After a few years spent in Middlesex county, he removed to a plantation about one-half mile south of the present village of Freneau, n
1 CONC ow known as Magnolia Farm. This eventually grew to about 400 acres.  He appears to have been very active in both church and worldy affairs and to have accumulated various properties in this and adjoining counties, including dock and store propertie
1 CONC s at Matawan. In his will he mentions his wife, Agnes. She was probably a second wife and not the mother of his children. He died in 1729, leaving sons; Peter (5), a merchant of Freehold, who died intestate in 1712; William (6); Richard (7), Gavi
1 CONC n (8), and David (9). There may have been daughters
1 CONT 
1 CONT William Watson (6), son of Peter died in 1751. He left two daughers- Euphemia (11), d 5-8-1770 aged 41  who married Michael Johnstone and Margaret (12) who does not seem to have married.
0 @N1057@ NOTE === History of the Watson Family ===
1 CONT Newspapers by Ancestry
1 CONT Article clipped from The Freehold Transcript and The Monmouth Inquirer
1 CONT Freehold, New Jersey- Friday June 26, 1931
1 CONT 
1 CONT -----------Watson is supposed to have been the father of John (2) and Peter (3) Watson.
1 CONT 
1 CONT John Watson (2) married Sarah Hidding. He was of Chingarora, where he diedi n 1708. But one child is mentioned, Abraham (4)
1 CONT 
1 CONT Peter Watson (3) landed on Staten Island, December 19, 1683, having been brought out under a four-year indenture. After a few years spent in Middlesex county, he removed to a plantation about one-half mile south of the present village of Freneau, n
1 CONC ow known as Magnolia Farm. This eventually grew to about 400 acres.  He appears to have been very active in both church and worldy affairs and to have accumulated various properties in this and adjoining counties, including dock and store propertie
1 CONC s at Matawan. In his will he mentions his wife, Agnes. She was probably a second wife and not the mother of his children. He died in 1729, leaving sons; Peter (5), a merchant of Freehold, who died intestate in 1712; William (6); Richard (7), Gavi
1 CONC n (8), and David (9). There may have been daughters
1 CONT 
1 CONT Captain Abraham Watson (4) son of John (2), diedin 1756. His tombstone is still to be seen in one of the graveyards in Middletown village. The name of his wife was Elisabeth. But one child is known, John (10)
1 CONT 
1 CONT William Watson (6), son of Peter died in 1751. He left two daughers- Euphemia (11), d 5-8-1770 aged 41  who married Michael Johnstone and Margaret (12) who does not seem to have married.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Richard Watson (7), son of Peter (3) died in 1736. The given name of his wife as Martha. Their children were: Peter (13), James (14), Richard (15), Ann (16), Margaret (17), Euphemia (18), Mary (19) and Jean (20) who married William Jolly.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Gavin Watson (8) son of Peter probably lived in Manalapan. His children were GavIN (21), Peter (22) of Freehold who died in 1761 having married Mary Ker, Euphemia (23) who married Joseph Ker, and Ann (24) who married Walter Ker.
1 CONT 
1 CONT David Watson (9), son of Peter (3) lived on the homestead at Mt Pleasant. His wife is thought to have been a daughter of Alexander Napier. His children are supposed to have been: David (25) who is thought to have married 1750 Ann Tice? and who ha
1 CONC d no children, Margaret (26), Agnes (27), mother of Philip Freneau, the Poet (1728-1817) who married first Pierre Fresneau and secondly Major James Kearney; Elizabeth (28) who married Zilpha Smith and Euphemia (29) who married Joseph Smith
1 CONT 
1 CONT John Watson (10) son of Capt Abraham (4) married Hope, daugher of John and Sarah (Hartshorne) Taylor. She was born 1721 and died, 
